Certainly! Below is a simple HTML and JavaScript implementation of a slider that calls the callback function `cb_sl1` whenever the slider value is changed or dragged. It also prints out the current value of the slider.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Slider Example</title>
<script>
function cb_sl1(value) {
    console.log("Slider value changed to: " + value);
}

function createSlider() {
    var slider = document.createElement("input");
    slider.type = "range";
    slider.min = "0";
    slider.max = "100";
    slider.value = "50";
    slider.oninput = function() {
        cb_sl1(this.value);
    };
    document.body.appendChild(slider);
}

window.onload = createSlider;
</script>
</head>
<body>
</body>
</html>
```

In this code, the `createSlider` function generates a range input element and sets its `oninput` event to call the `cb_sl1` function with the current value of the slider. The `cb_sl1` function then logs the value to the console.
